<section aria-labelledby="kubernetes-ai-example">
  <h2 id="kubernetes-ai-example">Why does this nginx pod keep restarting?</h2>
  <p>
    We started nginx in a local Kubernetes 1.37 cluster and pointed it at a config file
    the image didn’t contain. After four restarts, the pod showed <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>.
    Its <a href="#action-kubernetes.events_for_pod">warning event</a>
    said <code>Back-off restarting failed container nginx</code>. Kubernetes was giving
    the container more time between attempts. The reason nginx quit was in <a href="#action-kubernetes.pod_previous_logs">the previous container’s log</a>.
  </p>
  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Container log">nginx: [emerg] open() "/etc/site/nginx.conf" failed (2: No such file or directory)</.docs_code>
  <p>
    Another restart would use the same missing file. We removed the test deployment’s
    custom startup command so nginx would load its own config. The new pod came up with
    zero restarts and served the nginx welcome page.
  </p>
  <blockquote class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-brand-500 pl-5 text-zinc-200">
    Why is web restarting in emisar-lab? Check the pod events and the last container log.
    Show me what failed before I change the deployment.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    With the Kubernetes pack installed, an agent can make those checks through emisar.
    Pod events are low risk. Container logs are medium risk because applications can log
    private data. Full workload descriptions and YAML are high risk because they can
    include secrets. Set a <.link navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}>policy</.link>
    for each read you want to allow. You can require approval for a rollout or keep
    that change in your deployment repository.
  </p>
  <details class="mt-5 rounded-lg border border-zinc-800 px-4 py-3">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-zinc-100">
      Reproduce the failure with kubectl
    </summary>
    <p>
      Point kubectl at a disposable local cluster. Create a fresh namespace for this
      example and start the deployment with the bad path.
    </p>
    <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Create the test deployment" copy_id="k8s-crash-create">kubectl create namespace emisar-lab &amp;&amp;
kubectl -n emisar-lab create deployment web --image=nginx:1.28.0-alpine -- nginx -c /etc/site/nginx.conf -g 'daemon off;'</.docs_code>
    <p>
      Wait for the container to restart, then check the pod. Its name and restart count will vary.
    </p>
    <.docs_code
      phx-no-format
      label="Pod status"
      copy_text="kubectl -n emisar-lab get pods -l app=web"
    >$ kubectl -n emisar-lab get pods -l app=web
<span class="text-zinc-400">NAME                   READY   STATUS             RESTARTS      AGE
web-6c4c65bc88-xqqxm   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   4 (74s ago)   2m37s</span></.docs_code>
    <p>Use the pod name to look up its events. The BackOff warning records the failed restart.</p>
    <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Events for this pod" copy_id="k8s-crash-events">{~S|pod=$(kubectl -n emisar-lab get pod -l app=web -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl -n emisar-lab get events --field-selector involvedObject.kind=Pod,involvedObject.name="$pod"|}</.docs_code>
    <p>Read the previous log to see why nginx quit.</p>
    <.docs_code
      phx-no-format
      label="Previous log"
      copy_text="kubectl -n emisar-lab logs deployment/web --previous"
    >$ kubectl -n emisar-lab logs deployment/web --previous
<span class="text-zinc-400">2026/11/24 07:05:59 [emerg] 1#1: open() "/etc/site/nginx.conf" failed (2: No such file or directory)
nginx: [emerg] open() "/etc/site/nginx.conf" failed (2: No such file or directory)</span></.docs_code>
    <p>
      Remove the test command and wait for the replacement pod. When you finish,
      delete the namespace you created with <code>kubectl delete namespace emisar-lab</code>.
    </p>
    <.docs_code
      phx-no-format
      label="Restore the image’s startup command"
      copy_id="k8s-crash-restore"
    >{~S|kubectl -n emisar-lab patch deployment web --type=json -p='[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/command"}]'
kubectl -n emisar-lab rollout status deployment/web|}</.docs_code>
  </details>
</section>
